45
00:04:19,519 --> 00:04:30,000
Senior patrol leader: that's the key leadership position.

46
00:04:30,799 --> 00:04:35,280
Now, how do we work out the relationship that we have with our senior patrol?

47
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:38,719
You're the scout master. You're supposed to be watching what's going on,

48
00:04:38,719 --> 00:04:42,159
Supposed to mentor this young man through the process of learning to lead.

49
00:04:42,159 --> 00:04:44,719
Well, how does that happen? I got 10 points for you.

50
00:04:46,159 --> 00:04:48,000
One is to let him own the program.

51
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,320
I mean, they are responsible for the troop program.

52
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:54,080
They're responsible for meetings and outings,

53
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:55,440
They're responsible for the whole thing.

54
00:04:56,479 --> 00:05:01,200
If he's receiving a written plan and it's not actually responsible for the program,

55
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:04,960
he's responsible for running your idea of what the program should be.

56
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:11,280
Let him own it. Let him come up with some ideas,

57
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:13,599
Let him put them together. Let him run them.

58
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:15,840
He'll do a lot better than you think.

59
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:21,760
The other one is: never criticize your senior patrol leader publicly.

60
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:25,840
Don't holler at him. Don't criticize him in front of other scouts.

61
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:31,600
No other action is going to undercut his authority or the relationship that you

62
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,600
form with him than doing that.

63
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:38,480
Number three: give him some direction and some boundaries.

64
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:41,600
Keep them focused on what scouting promises.

65
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:43,600
Show them where the boundaries are,

66
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,240
But at the same time you do that,

67
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,600
point out the vast possibilities that exist within those boundaries.

68
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:55,840
Number four: be polite.

69
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:02,240
I'm not polite. sometimes I have to say and ask permission

70
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:07,360
If he's running something, if he's leading the scouts and something.

71
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:08,320
don't just barge in,

72
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:10,640
Ask permission.

73
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:13,680
Hey, can I take a moment to say something?

74
00:06:15,039 --> 00:06:15,840
Ask permission

75
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,479
Instead of jumping in and taking over and doing things.

76
00:06:20,479 --> 00:06:22,479
hey, do you need any adult support for that?

77
00:06:24,719 --> 00:06:27,440
Or what can I do to help you? do this

78
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:31,360
Quietly, not in front of the other scouts.

79
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:32,960
Be polite.

80
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,159
Number five is to encourage him.

81
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:39,520
There's always going to be problems and challenge is good.

82
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:43,760
He's responsible not to prevent the problems

83
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:45,200
but to lead the troop through them

84
00:06:46,719 --> 00:06:49,680
And problems, even if he creates them.

85
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:51,120
they're not punishable offenses.

86
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,919
They're good moments to learn.

87
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:59,120
Number six: set the proper tone for leadership.

88
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:01,520
Scouts aren't soldiers. you're not the captain.

89
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,359
The SPL is not the drill sergeant.

90
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,560
You're the coach. your senior patrol leader

91
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,960
is the team captain and the scouts are the players.

92
00:07:09,919 --> 00:07:12,000
Find out where the markings are on the field.

93
00:07:12,799 --> 00:07:14,000
Stay on the sidelines.

94
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:16,240
Let the SPL run the game.

95
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:20,719
Number seven is: use your authority sparingly.

96
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:23,120
First of all, you don't have a whole lot of authority anyway.

97
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,200
It's a good idea just to think that

98
00:07:26,799 --> 00:07:30,240
We have provisional authority where we can step in

99
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:33,120
and we can prevent unsafe or improper actions.

100
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,400
But the real authority lies in our youth leadership.

101
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:40,000
When circumstances demand, do this quietly.

102
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,760
Like I said, go back and ask permission

103
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:48,160
And see if you can quietly ask a lot of questions

104
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:49,760
that will lead to a course correction.

105
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:51,440
Don't grab the wheel out of their hands.

106
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:57,360
Don't hit the brake unless the wreck is imminent.

107
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:03,360
You know, ask some questions and guide them

108
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,800
towards a course correction.

109
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:07,360
Number eight: keep your distance.

110
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,320
Keep your distance,

111
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:13,280
Let the scouts live their own lives.

112
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,920
I mean: go fold a tent in the storeroom,

113
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,120
Go conduct a scout master's conference.

114
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,600
Go sit down, have a cup of coffee,

115
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,360
Watch quietly.

116
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:24,880
Go talk to your fellow leaders,

117
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:26,720
Stay out of their way,

118
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:28,880
Keep your distance,

119
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:29,920
Observe,

120
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:32,080
Observe,

121
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:35,520
But remember it's their place,

122
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:37,600
It's what they get to do,

123
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:39,600
It's their playing field.

124
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:41,760
And number nine follows up on that,

125
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,440
because we guard that playing field.

126
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:47,920
So we're not just coaches, sometimes we're referees

127
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:50,080
And when other adults interfere,

128
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,200
we've got to redirect their attention to something else.

129
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:56,640
Usually, one direct, clear statement

130
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:59,120
about the role of adults is all that you need to do,

131
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,600
And don't embarrass them, be polite.

132
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:05,680
But sometimes the ref has to step in and remind a coach

133
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,320
that he's on the field now and he needs to. you know,

134
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:09,840
hightail it for the sidelines.

135
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:15,520
Number 10, always give your senior patrol leader

136
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:16,480
plenty of feedback,

137
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:20,640
And plenty means about two minutes in every hour.

138
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:23,760
Don't wear them out.

139
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:28,080
Don't give him so much that, uh, you know

140
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:29,200
he can't process it.

141
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:32,720
Give him plenty of feedback about whatever's happening

142
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:33,200
right then,

143
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:36,480
Do it very, very quickly, quietly and politely

144
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,360
And then step back let him process it,

145
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:40,400
let him figure it out.

146
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:46,480
That's 10 little hints that have helped me

147
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:48,000
